<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jodhpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1226 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Jodhpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Marwar, the largest Rajput kingdom of the Thar, ruled by the Rathores from the fortress of Mehrangarh, whose warriors served and fought the Mughal emperors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores</a> trace their rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-siha">Rao Siha</a>, who settled near Pali in the 13th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-chunda">Rao Chunda</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a> around 1395, and in 1459 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and the hilltop fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh</a> as a new capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-maldeo">Rao Maldeo</a> (r. 1532–1562) made Marwar a regional power before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> defeated his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sammel">Sammel</a> in 1544. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> princes became Mughal commanders, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> tried to take Marwar after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaswant-singh-of-marwar">Jaswant Singh</a>'s death in 1678, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durgadas-rathore">Durgadas Rathore</a> led a long resistance. Marwari merchants spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as traders and bankers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> signed a treaty with the British in 1818. In 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanwant-singh">Hanwant Singh</a> briefly considered joining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> before acceding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in 1949 Jodhpur merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">Greater Rajasthan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jodhpur-state.jpg" alt="Jodhpur State" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: PD · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jodhpur.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1226 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jodhpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rao Jodha, Rao Maldeo, Jaswant Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marwari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marwar, Kingdom of Marwar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1588&amp;focus=jodhpur-state" aria-label="Jodhpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6608 1212 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jodhpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6928 1468L7018 1385M6928 1468L7364 1601M6928 1468L7364 1602M6928 1468L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6928 1468h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6928 1468h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jodhpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1588&amp;focus=jodhpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
